#Problem APEX 1.6.9

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Limits and continuity)
## DBsection(Infinite limits and vertical asymptotes)
## KEYWORDS('limit','infinite')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('1.6')
## Problem1('9')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$c    = random(1, 5) * random(-1, 1, 2);
$xmin = $c - 6;
$xmax = $c + 6;

$c_l = $c - 0.1;
$c_r = $c + 0.1;

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -8,
    xmax            =>  2,
    xtick_delta     =>  5,
    xminor          =>  4,
    ymin            => -2,
    ymax            =>  8,
    ytick_delta     =>  5,
    yminor          =>  4,
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_function("1/(x-$c)^2", 'x', -10, 10, color => 'blue');

$alt =
    "The graph of f(x)=1/(x-$c)^2. From the left, the graph increases as x "
    . "approaches $c and the graph extends past the plotting window. It then "
    . "decreases to the x-axis.";

$ans = Compute('Infinity');

BEGIN_PGML
>>[![$alt_text]!]{$plot}{400}<<

---
Evaluate the given limits using the graph of 
the function [` f(x) = \displaystyle \frac{1}{(x - [$c])^2}`] shown above.
  
a) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to [$c]^-} f(x)`] = [__]{$ans} 
  
b) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to [$c]^+} f(x)`] = [__]{$ans}
  
c) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to [$c]} f(x)`] = [__]{$ans}

[@ helpLink("limit") @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
There is a vertical asymptote at [`x = [$c]`], and the limits from either side are [` \infty`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
